WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.237
Just days before Finland

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:05.438
is set to become NATO's

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:06.539
newest member,

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:07.774
the government in Helsinki

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.542
is headed for a change.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.477
Results in Finland's

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:11.544
parliamentary elections

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:12.979
indicate prime ministers

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:15.682
on a marathon Center-Left Party will lose

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.151
and she will be out of office.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:19.853
She conceded defeat to the opposition

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.690
right wing National Coalition Party

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.293
its leader

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.628
Patriot, or

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:30.930
ran on a pro-business platform

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.099
and has vowed to cut spending

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.067
but his party will have

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.603
to form a coalition with other parties

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:37.670
to govern.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.773
Let's bring you more now on this.

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:41.775
I want to turn to Steven Erlanger.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:42.809
He's the chief diplomat

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.010
a correspondent for The New York Times,

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.379
and joins us now from Brussels via Skype.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:47.714
So good to see you.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.783
What led to the political downfall of MS.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:50.183
Marin?

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:51.951
Well, she

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.887
is still very popular in Finland,

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.222
but her Center-Left Party

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.391
was increasingly less popular.

00:00:58.391 --> 00:00:59.859
She was always more popular

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.828
than her party

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:03.863
and has been going on in nearby

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.665
Sweden also

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.700
where the last election saw

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.636
a swing to the right.

00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:10.670
That happened in Finland

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.840
to the Finns or kind of famously

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.175
tight fisted.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.078
And the biggest criticism of her

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:18.978
that mattered

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.580
domestically had nothing to do

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.382
with Ukraine.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:23.149
Or Nadeau,

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.217
in which she was considered

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:27.253
to have done a good job or even covered

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:30.023
but the size of the government debt.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.126
Now, in regular European terms,

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:36.930
Finnish debt is only about 70% of GDP.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.298
It's not bad at all.

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:39.432
But for the Finns,

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.367
it seemed much too much.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.770
So they have gone for

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:47.941
a Center-Right party very narrowly.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:48.408
It should be

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:50.176
said that promises

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.378
to rein in public spending

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:55.582
And that is really the problem.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.351
Son of a Marine during her campaign

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.353
just very aggressively

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:01.221
said that

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.489
the problem in Finland

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:04.224
was not enough growth

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:06.993
but refused to talk about budget cuts.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:10.163
So so this in the end cut against her.

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:12.265
But she does remain quite popular,

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.167
even if she's somewhat controversial

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.335
as well.

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:17.871
A fresh face, a new generation,

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:21.207
a woman who enjoys her private life

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:22.809
There's had a

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:23.710
controversy

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.646
in relatively conservative Finland,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.115
but I don't think was a very key issue

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:30.850
in her loss.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.519
Well, now the hard part starts there.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:32.986
Arguably,

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.321
a coalition will have to be formed.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.557
Could we see MS.

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:39.626
Marin make a comeback or

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:40.727
or play a role

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:42.662
in forming a new government?

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.997
I think very much so.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:46.065
I mean, it's a 200 seat parliament

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:48.768
and and the highest party got 48 seats.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.271
So it's going to have to be a coalition.

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:53.673
It's likely to be at least three parties.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.711
The sudden ruins problem is that her left

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.613
allies won't work

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.815
with the National Coalition Party.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:06.119
The big question for them is do they

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:09.122
go to the populist

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.223
harder right

00:03:10.223 --> 00:03:12.725
Finns party, which came in second

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.763
and build a coalition around that?

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.533
Or will they go more toward

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.570
the Social Democrats who came in a narrow

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.873
third and build a coalition around them?

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:27.907
And if they do that,

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.209
then it's possible, son,

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:31.744
a Marine would have another

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:33.846
ministerial office.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.448
She could be for a minister.

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:38.351
She she could be something else.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.919
I'm not sure that interests her

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.221
right now.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.656
One of the great questions in Finland is

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:47.026
what comes next for the son of a Marine?

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:49.229
And I think also the

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:49.462
rest of

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:49.963
the world

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:52.632
will be keenly watching her next move.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.935
Finland, as you know, is about to join.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:03:57.470
NATO has a decision

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.471
to join NATO's played.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:59.973
Any role in these elections?

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:00.873
I understand.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.041
I mean, it didn't even play a role.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.243
It wasn't contentious at all.

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:05.245
Did that surprise you?

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.280
Not exactly.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:07.814
I mean,

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.449
because foreign policy in Finland

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.451
is basically run by the president.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.620
It's a very powerful presidency.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.255
Solidness, though,

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:15.688
and he's

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:17.724
the most popular politician

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:19.459
in In Finland,

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:20.660
he was the one who kind of

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.494
really guided

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:25.398
Finland and Sweden to toward NATO's.

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:26.099
Now

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:29.936
Basically, all parties went along.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.538
Some on the left still didn't like it.

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:35.908
But as you say, it was not at that point

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:36.809
any longer.

00:04:37.744 --> 00:04:39.445
A controversial issue.

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:42.348
And by the time the election happened,

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:43.316
it was all done.

00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:44.517
I mean, all that remains

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:45.685
for Finland

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:46.052
to get in

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:48.554
NATO's exchange of a few letters.

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:50.623
So it really is a done deal.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:52.091
A done deal.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.026
Steven Erlanger,

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:55.161
thank you so much for providing us

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.462
with your insights greatly.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:56.996
Appreciate it.

